@*
    One of the game's conditions, indented the way the game indents it.

    A tick or a cross against each thing, and a heading over each group saying whether all of it or
    any of it has to hold. Run into a sentence instead, Corporate Dominion's own condition reads
    "not ethic Gestalt Consciousness and not authority Corporate and Oligarchic Authority and not
    Xenophobe or Fanatic Xenophobe" - four levels of nesting flattened into a word order that does
    not carry it, and no way for a reader to tell which "and" binds tighter.

    The things themselves are OptionChip, the same chip the empire list draws, so hovering one opens
    the panel saying what that ethic or civic actually does. A bare span was quicker to write and
    made the page the one place in the app where a chip is not worth hovering.

    Recursive, because the shape is. A group draws a heading and a list of itself; a leaf draws a
    mark and a chip. There is no depth limit: the deepest condition in the game is four, the
    outline drops the levels that say nothing, and a limit would silently hide the one bullet that
    explains why an option is closed.
*@

@if (Node.Join is ConditionJoin.Leaf)
{
    <li class="@(Node.Plain ? "plain" : Node.Wanted ? "wants" : "refuses")">
        @*
            The mark is decoration and the words beside it are not: a screen reader gets "needs" or
            "must not have", where a tick and a cross are two characters that sound alike or read as
            nothing at all.

            Neither, for a bullet that states something about the condition rather than naming
            something to hold - "must not have: never" is a sentence arguing with itself.
        *@
        @if (!Node.Plain)
        {
            <span class="mark" aria-hidden="true">@(Node.Wanted ? "✓" : "✗")</span>
            <span class="sem-visually-hidden">@(Node.Wanted ? "Needs" : "Must not have")</span>
        }

        @if (Node.Chip is { } chip)
        {
            @*
                Deferred, as the empire table's chips are. A civics page draws four hundred of these
                and binding a popover to each one up front is four hundred round trips into
                JavaScript before the reader has hovered anything.
            *@
            <OptionChip Key="@chip.Key" Name="@chip.Name" Icon="@chip.Icon"
                        DescriptionKey="@chip.Description" Effects="@chip.Effects"
                        Deferred="true" />
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="words">@Node.Text</span>
        }
    </li>
}
else
{
    <li class="group">
        <span class="join">@(Node.Join is ConditionJoin.All ? "All of" : "Any of")</span>

        <ul>
            @foreach (var part in Node.Parts)
            {
                <ConditionBullets Node="@part" />
            }
        </ul>
    </li>
}

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The condition, or the part of one, to draw.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required ConditionOutline Node { get; set; }
}
